The three operations, all executed with a real SQLite database:

```go
db, _ := sql.Open("sqlite", ":memory:")
db.Exec("CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, email TEXT)")

// 1. Exec — INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (no rows back)
result, err := db.Exec("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)", "Al", "al@x.com")
// ? = parameter — SAFE against injection — verified

// 2. Query — SELECT (iterate rows)
rows, err := db.Query("SELECT id, name, email FROM users")
defer rows.Close()  // ALWAYS — rows is a resource
for rows.Next() {
    var id int
    var name, email string
    rows.Scan(&id, &name, &email)
    // each row — verified: id=1, name=Al, email=al@x.com
}

// 3. QueryRow — exactly one row
var email string
db.QueryRow("SELECT email FROM users WHERE name = ?", "Al").Scan(&email)
// email = al@x.com — verified
```

The injection rule, the most-tested point:

```go
// BAD — string concatenation
query := "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + userInput + "'"
// user input: ' OR '1'='1 → everything leaks / '); DROP TABLE users; -- → disaster

// GOOD — parameterized
db.Query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", userInput)
// The driver sends the query and the value separately — the value can never become SQL
```

The lifecycle rules:
- db is a POOL — Open once (main/init), close on shutdown
- rows must Close — always defer
- Scan checks column count/types — Scan into properly typed variables
- errors.ErrNoRows for QueryRow's 'not found' — the sentinel to check
